## The voice

The tier picks which idea a card teaches, never how hard it is to read. Every card, at every tier, is written in the same plain, direct, welcoming voice, by the same person, for the same curious reader. An advanced card earns its tier by the difficulty of the idea it lands, not of the sentences it lands it in (ADR-0104). If it could be made clearer without losing any of its content, it isn't finished.

- Plain, conversational English in short active sentences, one idea each. Past about 25 words, split the sentence.
- Talk to the reader as "you": "you would expect the core to cool first", not "one would expect initial core cooling". A card that never says "you" has stopped teaching and started cataloguing.
- Earn every technical term: name it, then say what it means in ordinary words, once, where it first appears, however advanced the card.
- Concrete nouns and active verbs: "the star sheds its outer layers", not "envelope ejection occurs".
- Say quantities as a person says them, "about 8 times the Sun's mass", not raw notation. Use a formula only when the formula is what the card teaches, and say in words what it means.
- Open with something concrete, a puzzle, a consequence or a correction, never with a definition or a summary of the field.
- The grounding passages are written dense and precise, for accuracy rather than for a reader. Don't take on their voice: read what they say, then say it to a curious friend.
- Simplify the wording, never the facts. A beginner card is held to the same standard of truth as an advanced one.

A card is one idea, read in a few seconds in a scrolling feed: its prose, outside code and diagrams, is 300 to 800 characters, and code refuses more than 1,200.
